{"i":0,"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"92f2c1","wall_time_seconds":2.363848125,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":2682,"output":"{"body":"**Blocking: cancellation here persistently wedges the ref.**\n\n`exec.CommandContext`'s default cancel is `Process.Kill()` (SIGKILL), and no `Cancel`/`WaitDelay` is set. `CompareAndSwapRef` holds the transaction *prepared* — git owns `.git/refs/\u003cname\u003e.lock` — across the `git symbolic-ref` fork before committing. The root ctx cancels on SIGINT/SIGTERM (`main.go:44-66`), and the pre-push hook runs under it, so Ctrl-C during `git push` lands in that window.\n\nReproduced on git 2.50.1:\n\n| cancel | `.lock` left | later plain `git update-ref` | txn |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SIGKILL (current) | **yes** | **fails: cannot lock ref … File exists** | aborted |\n| SIGTERM | no | succeeds | aborted |\n| close stdin | no | succeeds | aborted |\n\nGit has no stale ref-lock recovery — its own message says \"remove the file manually to continue\". The OPF path takes the lock once per ref, so a migration backfill has N windows.\n\nTwo things that may help: **SIGTERM is sufficient** (git's tempfile signal cleanup removes the lock), so `procutil.TerminateOnCancel(cmd)` fixes it in one line with an existing helper. And no variant silently commits — I checked the ref value after each, all three abort — so this is purely lock hygiene, not a spurious write. Closing stdin also works but exits 0, which the Go side would then need to not read as success.","created_at":"2026-09-02T09:06:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3912492047","line":84,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/gitrepo/ref_cas.go"}
