{"body":"This changes the gate from deadline-gated to cancelability-gated, which is the opposite of what the comment being deleted promised: it said a deadline-free ctx \"takes the same blocking kernel path as Acquire, **so existing callers keep their exact behavior**\", and named turn-end condensation as a must-wait caller.\n\nEvery CLI context is cancelable (root ctx, `main.go:44`), so in practice only `context.Background()` now reaches the kernel path. Turn-end condensation now does 25ms `LOCK_NB` polling.\n\nThe part I'd raise loudest is fairness rather than the syscalls: `LOCK_EX` gives you a kernel queue position, poll-and-retry gives you none, so under the N-agents-one-repo workflow a poller can be starved indefinitely. This is a shared primitive affecting every caller, and no commit message mentions the change — worth either scoping it to the callers that need it or making it an explicit, documented decision.","created_at":"2026-09-02T09:06:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3912492063","line":null,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/internal/flock/flock_unix.go"}
{"body":"**This replaces my last finding with an unbounded, uninterruptible wait on the same path — and the flock it waits for is not what makes the fix work.**\n\nThe CLI root context (`main.go:29`) is cancelable but carries no deadline, so `lockFile` takes the blocking `syscall.Flock(LOCK_EX)` branch, which never consults ctx. The holder is `updatePersistentRef`, which keeps this same lock across its whole build-and-CAS loop — up to `shadowRefMaxRetries` = 16 checkpoint-tree rebuilds — and the lock file lives in the git **common** dir, so a sibling worktree contends on the same v1 ref name.\n\nScenario: condensation takes the v1 flock in worktree 1 and starts an expensive build (redaction dominates there). A `git push` in worktree 2 reaches `atomicSetV1Ref` -\u003e `CASPersistentRef` and blocks in the kernel. Ctrl-C prints `Interrupting… press Ctrl-C again to force quit` and does nothing; only a second Ctrl-C escapes. No message explains the stall.\n\nConfirmed: holding the flock and calling `CASPersistentRef` with a `context.WithCancel(context.Background())` — exactly main.go's shape — then cancelling gives `still blocked 3s after cancel`.\n\n**And the flock earns almost nothing.** The condensation path already used native `git update-ref`, so the #2204 lost update came purely from OPF's `CheckAndSetReference`; routing OPF through native CAS is the entire fix. This flock only covers the window between its own acquire and the CAS, but `expectedOld` was read at the start of the rewrite, long before the lock — so a condensation landing mid-rewrite yields a conflict either way. Removing the flock from `CASPersistentRef` leaves all three OPF regression tests passing (`TestOPFV1RewriteDoesNotLoseConcurrentCheckpointUpdate`, `TestOPFCheckpointRefRewriteDoesNotLoseConcurrentCheckpointUpdate`, `TestAtomicSetV1Ref_ReportsActualCASConflictAsMoved`); the only failure is `TestCASPersistentRef_WaitsForHeldFlockWithoutCallerDeadline`, which exists to describe the flock itself.\n\nSuggest dropping it here and relying on native CAS plus `retryPersistentRefLockContention` (16 attempts x ~10-25ms, so bounded well under a second). If it is kept for retry-churn reasons it needs a bound **and** a soft classification, so a timeout leaves the refs queued rather than aborting the user's push, plus a stderr line so the wait is not silent.","created_at":"2026-09-03T13:59:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309134","line":null,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go"}
{"body":"**This substring also matches three permanent, non-concurrency failures, and one route is a diagnosis regression against main.**\n\nReproduced on git 2.50.1, all classifying as `conflict=true`:\n\n| planted condition | git stderr |\n| --- | --- |\n| corrupt loose ref | `cannot lock ref 'refs/probe/x': unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/x': reference broken` |\n| unreadable ref (mode 000) | `… unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/y': Permission denied` |\n| name is a directory | `… unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/z'` |\n\nVia `casUpdateRef` these become `ErrShadowRefBusy`, so `updatePersistentRef` rebuilds the checkpoint tree and retries **16 times** on the turn-end hook before failing with \"shadow branch ref busy\" — wrong cause, 16x the work. Via `atomicSetV1Ref` they become `V1RefMovedError`, so a corrupt or unreadable v1 ref aborts the push with a concurrency story. That second one is a regression against main, where `storage.ErrReferenceHasChanged` would not fire for these and the real cause surfaced.\n\n`TestCASPersistentRef_DoesNotClassifyRefNamespaceConflictAsContention` covers the D/F case in the other direction (creating `parent/child` when `parent` exists yields `'…' exists; cannot create '…'` and classifies fine). The direction that breaks is the untested one.\n\nThe genuine \"deleted concurrently\" case has no trailing reason (verified: `unable to resolve reference 'refs/probe/gone'` and nothing more), so requiring the absence of a tail separates the first two rows. For the third, gate `V1RefMovedError` in `atomicSetV1Ref` on the re-read actually differing from `expectedOld` — it already does that re-read to fill `Actual`.","created_at":"2026-09-03T13:59:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309148","line":null,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/gitrepo/ref_cas.go"}
{"body":"`WaitDelay` is left at zero. Go's docs recommend setting it precisely for a custom `Cancel` that does not kill, because otherwise `Wait` blocks indefinitely on both the process and the stderr copier. `cmd.Stderr` is a `bytes.Buffer`, so os/exec allocates a pipe plus a copier goroutine that `Wait` joins — any grandchild inheriting that pipe holds `Wait` open even after git exits.\n\nA blocking `reference-transaction` hook (git runs it at prepared/committed/aborted) reproduces the same wedge inside the child: git never exits on EOF, `tx.wait()` never returns, and the hook hangs with the transaction prepared and the `.lock` held — with the root context's cancel unable to break it.\n\nPlausible rather than confirmed; I reasoned it from os/exec semantics and git's hook points without staging a blocking hook. A few seconds of `cmd.WaitDelay` covers it.","created_at":"2026-09-03T13:59:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309156","line":103,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/gitrepo/ref_cas.go"}
{"body":"Worth adding to the PR description: `casUpdateRef` is the shared entry point, so this reroutes more than the two OPF writers the description names — `casUpdateShadowBranchRef` (`checkpoint/shadow_ref.go`) and every `updatePersistentRef` caller go through it too, which puts it on the per-checkpoint shadow-branch write in the Stop hook.\n\nBenchmarked at 60 iterations on a 400-commit repo: the old single `git update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003cnew\u003e \u003cold\u003e` fork is 13.4ms/op, the new transaction plus the `symbolic-ref` probe is 22.0ms/op — +8.6ms per ref update, and `updatePersistentRef` can retry up to 16 times. Against the ~3s p50 hook floor that is under 1%, so not a blocker, just an unmentioned scope expansion onto a path this repo watches.\n\nI checked the obvious way to drop the extra fork and it does not work: `symref-verify \u003cref\u003e` alongside `update \u003cref\u003e …` in one transaction fails with `fatal: prepare: multiple updates for ref '…' not allowed` on both backends. So the separate fork is a legitimate workaround, not an oversight.","created_at":"2026-09-03T13:59:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/2211#discussion_r3925309164","line":26,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/persistent_ref_update.go"}
